; hx711_device.loom
; An HX711 emulation: a device on the HX711's two-wire interface (PD_SCK
; in, DOUT out) that answers with readings the host sets, one per input
; (channel A gain 128, channel B gain 32, channel A gain 64), honours the
; pulse count of each read for the next conversion's input, reports which
; input each reading was, and powers down on a long PD_SCK high. A test
; source for HX711 host firmware, hx711_host.loom among it.
;
; Written on 2026-11-05, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035): the HX711 interface is in no
; Loom design document, and it is not SPI (the clock count after the data
; selects the next input, and a long clock high powers the chip down). The
; program uses:
;   - the bit engine's shift register in manual mode (SHO, SEMANTICS 6.9),
;     built for the synchronous serial programs: one SHO a bit puts the
;     next bit on DOUT, and CNT counts the 24 bits;
;   - LD/ST of slice B (6.11), built for the I2C EEPROM's array: the three
;     host-set readings live in six words of the instruction memory;
;   - the timed waits (WAITP ... T, 6.4) and a CSRW TD that puts a saved
;     deadline back (6.6), so one deadline register times both the
;     conversion and the 60 us power-down watchdog;
;   - the FIFOs for the readings in and the reports out.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the ready/busy line, the
; 24-bit frame across two loads of the 16-bit shift register, the pulse
; count and the input it selects, the conversion time, the power-down
; detection and the reset after it.
;
; Weaker than the chip. (1) DOUT follows a PD_SCK rise 9 to 12 clocks
; later (13 to 16 for bit 15, the one after the shift register is
; reloaded): the WAITP sees the rise through the pad synchroniser and
; the slot grid, then one SHO and the pad register. The datasheet's T2 is
; at most 0.1 us, 5 clocks at 50 MHz, so this device needs a host that
; samples DOUT at least 16 clocks (0.32 us at 50 MHz) after the rise, as
; hosts that sample at the end of the high time or after the fall do,
; and hx711_host.loom does. (2) Each bit is six slots, so PD_SCK needs a
; period of at least 24 clocks (0.48 us at 50 MHz; the datasheet allows
; 0.4 us), and high and low at least 10 clocks each, the datasheet's 0.2
; us at 50 MHz (measured in the tests). (3) The conversion time is a
; host-set number of ticks from the end of the read's pulses to DOUT
; low, not the chip's free-running 10 or 80 samples/s: the RTL budget
; cannot hold 100 ms. (4) No settling: a reading is ready one conversion
; time after a read or a wake, where the chip needs four (Table 2,
; settling time). (5) Pulses 26 and 27 are caught at the bit loop's pace
; only if each comes within GAP = 64 ticks of the fall before it (64 us at
; a 1 us tick; T3's 50 us maximum high plus any low under 14 us fits); a
; later one, and a 28th, are counted by the conversion loop, which needs a
; PD_SCK period of about 60 clocks to see them (the tests' 28-pulse host
; runs at 100). The read's pulses end at the first gap, and the conversion
; time is counted from there.
;
; Source: Avia Semiconductor, "HX711 24-Bit Analog-to-Digital Converter
; (ADC) for Weigh Scales", sections "Serial Interface" and "Reset and
; Power-Down", Table 3, Fig. 2 and the timing table under it, Fig. 3;
; the facts used are listed in hx711_host.loom's header. What this device
; takes for the cases the datasheet leaves open:
;   - pulses before DOUT goes low (while converting) count with the read's
;     pulses 25 to 27, so they make the count wrong, and a count other
;     than 25, 26 or 27 ("should not be less than 25 or more than 27",
;     a "serial communication error") selects channel A gain 128, the
;     reset input, and is reported as an error;
;   - a high of more than PD ticks powers it down at any point, in a read
;     or not; PD = 60 ticks, 60 to 61 us at the suggested 1 us tick; the
;     datasheet's 50 us maximum high never does;
;   - DOUT keeps its level while powered down (the datasheet does not
;     say); on the return low the device resets (input A/128), drives
;     DOUT high and converts.
;
; Pins: PD_SCK = IN1, DOUT = OUT1 (push-pull, high at start). Run beside
; hx711_host.loom (thread 0: PD_SCK = OUT0, DOUT = IN0), the bench wiring
; OUT0 to IN1 and OUT1 to IN0.
;
; Rate: the host's PD_SCK times the bits; the tick (host-set, the M2
; convention; TICK_INT 50, 1 us, at 50 MHz) times the conversion and the
; power-down watchdog only. There are no deadline pairs.
;
; Host commands (INQ[1]), taken at the end of every conversion, before
; DOUT goes low, so a reading set now is served from the next one:
;     0x8000 | I << 12 | H, L   the reading for input I (0 A/128, 1 B/32,
;                   2 A/64; 3 is ignored): H its bits 23:16, then a second
;                   word L its bits 15:0. Push the two together. The
;                   readings are 0 after load.
;     0x4000 | N    the conversion time: N ticks, 0..0x3FFF (0: DOUT low
;                   at once). Default CONV0 = 100.
; Reports (OUTQ[1]), one each time a reading is made ready (DOUT low):
;     bits 1:0      the input this reading is of (the selection of the read
;                   before it, 0 after start or a wake)
;     bits 12:8     that read's pulse count, bits 4:0 of it (25 after
;                   start or a wake: the count that selects A/128)
;     bit 15        the count was not 25, 26 or 27 (input A/128 taken)
;     0x4000        alone: the device has powered down (on the long high)
; A report that finds OUTQ full is dropped.
;
; Registers: r0 scratch and the report; r1 the bit count and table
; address; r2 the saved deadline and scratch; r3 bits 15:0 of the reading;
; r4 the pulse count; r5 the input now converting; r6 the conversion
; time; r7 the error bit (and .csr scratch at start).
;
; Memory: thread 1's quarter (.imem 512): 118 words of code from 0x080 to
; 0x0F5 and the six reading words at 0x0FA. With hx711_host.loom (91 words
; from 0x000) the pair fits threads 0 and 1 and leaves 2 and 3 free.
;
;
; Decided at integration (2026-11-05): Fig. 2's "CH.B, Gain:64" for 27 pulses is read
; as channel A, gain 64, as Table 3 and the text say; the conversion time is
; host-set (the real 10 or 80 samples/s do not fit an RTL run); the device
; changes DOUT 9 to 16 clocks after a PD_SCK rise, not within 0.1 us, and
; needs a PD_SCK period of 24 clocks or more: stated limits of a firmware
; device, met by hx711_host and by any host that samples late in the high.
        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 1
        .pins   SCK = IN1, DOUT = OUT1
        .tick   50                      ; 1 us at 50 MHz (no pairs: timeouts only)
        .equ    PD = 60                ; ticks of high that power it down
        .equ    CONV0 = 100             ; the default conversion time, ticks
        .equ    GAP = 64                ; ticks of low that end a read's pulses
        .equ    TBL = 0x0FA             ; 6 words: H << 8, L for inputs 0..2

start:  .csr    BE_PINS, pin(DOUT)      ; SHO drives DOUT (clobbers r7)
        .csr    BE_CFG, 2               ; DIR = 1: MSB first (clobbers r7)
        LDI     r6, CONV0
reset:  SETP    DOUT, 1                 ; busy
        LDI     r5, 0                   ; channel A, gain 128
        LDI     r4, 25                  ; as though 25 pulses had chosen it

; Converting: DOUT high until TD. A rise counts a pulse; while it is high
; TD is the watchdog, then the conversion's deadline goes back.
convst: CSRR    r2, NOW
        ADD     r2, r2, r6
        CSRW    TD, r2                  ; the conversion ends at NOW + r6
conv:   WAITP   SCK, 1, T
        BT      done
        INC     r4                      ; a pulse
        CSRR    r2, TD
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
        CSRW    TD, r2                  ; (if it passed, the next WAITP ends at once)
        JMP     conv

; The conversion is over: the input it was of, the report, the commands.
done:   LDI     r7, 0
        MOV     r5, r4
        SUBI    r5, 25                  ; C = (count >= 25); 25, 26, 27 -> 0, 1, 2
        BNC     bad
        CMPI    r5, 3                   ; C = (count >= 28)
        BNC     rpt
bad:    LDI     r5, 0                   ; a wrong count: A/128 ...
        LDIH    r7, 0x80                ; ... and the error bit
rpt:    MOV     r0, r4
        ANDI    r0, 31
        SHLI    r0, 8
        OR      r0, r0, r5
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        CALL    report                  ; leaves TD = NOW, so the WAITB below only tests
drain:  WAITB   INQ_NE, T
        BT      load
        POP     r0
        MOV     r1, r0
        SHRI    r1, 15
        BNZ     setr
        SHLI    r0, 2                   ; 0x4000 | N
        SHRI    r0, 2
        MOV     r6, r0                  ; (N = 0: DOUT low at once)
        JMP     drain
setr:   MOV     r1, r0
        SHRI    r1, 12
        ANDI    r1, 3                   ; I
        SHLI    r0, 8                   ; H << 8
        POP     r3                      ; L (pushed with the first word)
        CMPI    r1, 3
        BZ      drain                   ; I = 3: ignored
        SHLI    r1, 1
        LDI     r2, TBL
        ADD     r1, r1, r2
        ST      r0, r1, 0
        ST      r3, r1, 1
        JMP     drain

; Ready: the reading of input r5 loaded, DOUT low, then the 24 bits. Each
; bit is the rise (WAITP), the SHO that puts it on DOUT, the watchdog for
; the high, the fall: six slots. CNT counts 24, r1 the first 8.
load:   MOV     r1, r5
        SHLI    r1, 1
        LDI     r2, TBL
        ADD     r1, r1, r2
        LD      r0, r1, 0               ; bits 23:16, in SR's top byte
        LDSR    r0
        LD      r3, r1, 1               ; bits 15:0, loaded after bit 16
        LDI     r0, 24
        CSRW    CNT, r0
        LDI     r1, 8
        LDI     r4, 25                  ; the count, as pulse 25 will make it
        SETP    DOUT, 0                 ; ready
b1:     WAITP   SCK, 1                  ; bits 23..16
        SHO
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
        DJNZ    r1, b1
        WAITP   SCK, 1                  ; bit 15: SR reloaded first
        LDSR    r3
        SHO
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
b3:     WAITP   SCK, 1                  ; bits 14..0
        SHO
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
        BNZ     b3                      ; Z from the SHO: CNT reached 0
; Pulses 25 to 27, at the bit loop's pace (six slots a pulse): each block
; waits GAP ticks for its rise; a gap ends the read with the count so far.
        WAITP   SCK, 1                  ; pulse 25: DOUT back high
        SETP    DOUT, 1
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
        SETD    GAP
        WAITP   SCK, 1, T               ; pulse 26?
        BT      c25
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown
        SETD    GAP
        WAITP   SCK, 1, T               ; pulse 27?
        BT      c26
        SETD    PD
        WAITP   SCK, 0, T
        BT      pdown                   ; any more are counted while converting
c27:    INC     r4                      ; r4 is 25 from `load`
c26:    INC     r4
c25:    JMP     convst

; PD_SCK high for PD ticks: powered down until it falls, then a reset.
pdown:  LDI     r0, 0
        LDIH    r0, 0x40
        CALL    report
        WAITP   SCK, 0
        JMP     reset

; Push r0 if OUTQ has room, else drop it. Never blocks.
report: SETD    0
        WAITB   OUTQ_NF, T
        BT      rfull
        PUSH    r0
rfull:  RET

        .org    TBL
        .word   0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0        ; the readings (host-set)
